# Checked authority, caching and reuse · `spec:nrz:reuse-concept`

**Principle (Reuse preserves an explicitly scoped claim)** · `prin:nrz:reuse-claim`

Reuse replaces work by a reference to already admitted authority for the same claim and dependencies. Its contract identifies the retained object, equality relation, lifetime owner, publication boundary, remaining checks and resource charges. A lookup does not establish a mathematical fact, a shared schema does not identify two values, and a retained symbolic proof does not certify a new numerical operating point. The graph of authority and dependencies determines which work may be omitted; a choice of container does not determine that authority.

**Caveat (Concept and implementation evidence)** · `cav:nrz:reuse-scope`

This concept defines obligations common to reuse mechanisms. Its instance statements cite their owning contracts; a specified recipe is not thereby implemented. An implicit field remains an open requirement under its own label. No capacity, allowance, default, literal cost or implementation is changed by these definitions, and no unmeasured accounting defect follows from an unfinished obligation.

## Authority and lifetime · `sec:nrz:reuse-taxonomy`

**Definition (Lemma)** · `def:nrz:reuse-lemma`

A lemma is checked mathematical authority for an exact judgment under explicit premises and a fixed rule interpretation. Its authority consists of that judgment and its checked evidence, including all residual hypotheses; a conditional lemma does not discharge its own assumptions. A named lemma has a stable schema name and revision whose checked applications retain their parameters and premises. An anonymous lemma has content identity instead of a caller-assigned name. A registered primitive supplies the checker's declared foundation; a derived lemma retains a checked derivation over that foundation. A display name, cached value or successful search flag supplies no additional authority (`rule:nrz:cas-conditional-witnesses`), (`def:nrz:reuse-lambda`).

**Definition (Anonymous lemma as a lambda)** · `def:nrz:reuse-lambda`

Let Δ be an ordered typed parameter telescope, Γ an ordered telescope of premise judgments over Δ, J the exact conclusion, D the complete immutable dependency closure, and b a checked derivation of J from Γ over Δ and D. An anonymous lemma is the closure `L = close_D(λ(x:Δ). λ(p:Γ(x)). b(x,p))`, of type `Π(x:Δ). Π(p:Γ(x)). J(x)`. The notation permits dependent parameters and premises; it binds every variable in b, Γ and J not defined in D. Closing D recursively binds its declarations, exact constants, rule/schema revisions and cited authority, in acyclic dependency order, so no unresolved name, mutable environment or ambient owner lookup is a free variable of L. The empty parameter telescope gives a ground lemma; checking one ground instance never licenses quantification over unproved instances. The lambda is a mathematical proof abstraction, not a promise of a general executable closure API (`conv:nrz:reuse-canonical-form`), (`rule:nrz:reuse-application`).

**Definition (Inline derivation)** · `def:nrz:reuse-inline`

An inline derivation is a checked proof fragment constructed at its use site without publishing a separately retained lemma node for that fragment. It has the same premise, scope and checker obligations as a retained proof and may cite existing lemmas as premises. Its conclusion is a proof fact, but subsequent reuse of that fact requires retained checked authority or another inline derivation. In particular a trivial identity can borrow an already prepared constant instead of repeating constant preparation (`[CAS-rule:cas:constant-cache-identities]`).

**Definition (Index or memo)** · `def:nrz:reuse-index`

An index maps a complete key to candidate node references; a memo records a previously computed lookup or other nonauthoritative result. Neither creates checked authority. A successful lookup must resolve and authenticate its retained target under the current owner, generation, complete identity and applicable limits. A hash selects candidates and complete equality resolves collisions. An index may be discarded or rebuilt without changing the judgments of surviving evidence; retaining an index entry cannot keep a dead authority valid. Negative or search-result memos depend on the search snapshot, policy and allowance that produced them and cannot turn exhaustion into mathematical falsity (`rule:nrz:reuse-node-lifetime`), (`rule:nrz:reuse-work`).

**Definition (Shared encoding authority)** · `def:nrz:reuse-encoding`

Shared encoding is immutable canonical data retained once and referenced by several records. Its authority is only that the referenced bytes encode the specified content under the specified format revision. Segmentation and allocation sharing preserve the complete logical byte sequence and its equality; pointer equality is not semantic equality. This permits omitting repeated copies or encodings of those bytes, while fresh framing, references, comparisons and admission obligations remain. Sharing bytes establishes no mathematical truth beyond any independently checked authority that owns them (`[CAS-dec:cas:registration-key-segment]`).

**Definition (Checked schema authority)** · `def:nrz:reuse-schema`

Checked schema authority is an immutable validated declaration environment or sealed instantiation recipe. It states which parameterized declaration records may be formed under its complete snapshot, descriptor and policy dependencies. It may retain shared encoding, but a byte segment alone does not authorize instantiation. Applying a recipe still checks its actual arguments and creates the required fresh identities and ordered relations. This authority proves declaration admissibility; it supplies neither an equation identifying two fresh values, a numerical bound nor source independence (`[CAS-rule:cas:frozen-environment-admission]`), (`[BINDINGS-rule:bindings:quantity-registration-freshness]`).

**Definition (Published provenance)** · `def:nrz:reuse-provenance`

Published provenance is the immutable operation or declaration record that retains the lifetime owner of its evidence and records the evidence's original dependencies. It is a route to existing authority, not a second checker or duplicate proof cache. A result can become numerically stale while its provenance still owns a valid symbolic theorem about the recorded declarations. Such retention does not grant a current attachment to mutated numerical values. Only completed publication creates reusable provenance under the operation's declared boundary (`def:nrz:operation-record`), (`[MATRIX-rule:matrix:quantity-covariance-proof-reuse]`).

**Definition (Numerical artifact)** · `def:nrz:reuse-numerical-artifact`

A numerical artifact is a computed value, factor, evaluated Jacobian, enclosure, residual or numerical certificate whose claim also depends on actual input regions and versions, coordinate maps, precision, arithmetic/algorithm choices, error policy and the relevant source/companion evidence. Its dependency closure is determined by that claim: an operator factor and a complete solve certificate have different closures. Checked symbolic authority can be a dependency without covering these numerical dependencies. Reuse must revalidate every dependency of the numerical claim and retain the associated tracked error (`rule:nrz:computation-cache-validity`), (`rule:nrz:computation-result-cache`).

**Definition (Local retention)** · `def:nrz:reuse-local-lifetime`

Local retention makes an admitted object eligible for citation within its authenticated owner and valid namespace generation while that owner retains it. Destruction, eviction, rollback or replacement can end eligibility according to that mechanism's contract. An immutable result already owned by a published consumer can survive removal of the index or retained slot that led to it. Local graph handles do not transfer authority to another store (`[CAS-rule:cas:anonymous-judgment-locality]`).

**Definition (Portable proof)** · `def:nrz:reuse-portable-lifetime`

A portable proof is a self-contained canonical encoding of a judgment and its derivation over declared primitive revisions. Its bytes can outlive the producing store; accepting them in a receiving store requires that store's independent checker and limits. Retaining a successful replay can skip later replay only after local authentication and complete key matching. Portable equality transports content, never a producing owner's live handles or attachments (`rule:nrz:cas-conditional-witnesses`), (`[CAS-thm:cas:lemma-cache]`).

**Definition (Live attachment)** · `def:nrz:reuse-live-lifetime`

A live attachment connects evidence to current native values, regions and companions under a validated owner lease. Its lifetime includes every captured numerical version and source dependency. Invalidation or retirement ends that authority even if an archived theorem and its portable bytes remain valid. Equal freshly observed values cannot revive a retired attachment; publication of new native authority requires a new complete capture, containment check and lease (`rule:nrz:lattice-attachment-lifetime`), (`dec:nrz:lattice-widened-revalidation`).

**Rule (Choice of proof form)** · `rule:nrz:reuse-proof-choice`

Every proof fact is justified by a named lemma application, an anonymous lemma citation or an inline derivation. Choose among sound forms using the declared cost policy, including key construction, premise checking, storage and reuse opportunities. A cheap sign consequence can remain inline when forming a portable proof key costs more than deriving it. Once a matching exact preparation is retained and eligible, a citation uses its checked payload; it does not secretly repeat the omitted preparation. Neither choosing a cheaper form nor retaining a shortcut erases a hypothesis or changes the claim (`dec:nrz:cas-path-cost`), (`[CAS-thm:cas:lemma-cache]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:constant-cache-identities]`).

## Content identity and application · `sec:nrz:reuse-lambda-identity`

**Convention (Canonical content form)** · `conv:nrz:reuse-canonical-form`

Write `Can_v` for the format-revision-v canonical encoding of a typed finite dependency DAG. It uses disjoint judgment/operator tags, explicit field and sequence framing, exact canonical scalar encodings, scoped binder references invariant under bound-variable renaming, and complete declaration identities. Ordered operands, parameters, premises, coordinates and proof steps retain their order; only collections declared to be sets use canonical sorting. Dependencies are encoded child before parent from declared ordered roots with canonical structural sharing and collision-resolving equality. A reference abbreviates the complete immutable referenced content, not its address. Source occurrences and declaration identities remain distinct wherever the claim depends on them. Canonical form does not quotient by algebraic equality, erase domains, reorder arbitrary premises, or identify different proof routes. These rules specialise the structural and witness conventions of (`def:nrz:cas-design-witness-format`) without requiring expansion of a shared DAG into a tree.

**Definition (Statement key and proof key)** · `def:nrz:reuse-content-keys`

Partition D into the complete immutable dependencies of the judgment and parameter/premise types, D_J, and any additional dependencies of its chosen derivation, D_b. Include the judgment kind, semantic/checker revisions, representation, original target, context, stratum and all residual obligations in the exact judgment J. The statement key is `K_J = Can_v(kind, revisions, Δ, Γ, J, D_J)`; the proof key is `K_π = Can_v(K_J, D_b, b)`. Every dependency omitted from K_J because it belongs only to the selected proof remains retained and authenticated as part of the cited proof's closure. A local index may use interned references for these contents only within its authenticated immutable namespace; its effective lookup key includes owner and generation. A portable key encodes complete graph-independent contents and a receiving cache separately authenticates its own proof owner. Neither key is a value, an approximate endpoint, a printed name, a raw address or an unchecked digest (`conv:nrz:reuse-canonical-form`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:anonymous-judgment-key]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:constant-cache-key]`).

**Definition (The same lambda)** · `def:nrz:reuse-lambda-equality`

Two checked lambdas are the same proof lambda exactly when their complete canonical closure contents agree: `L₁ ≡_λ L₂` means `K_π(L₁) = K_π(L₂)` under the same canonical-form revision. Equality of K_J instead identifies the same parameterized judgment, written `L₁ ≡_J L₂`, and can hold for distinct derivations. Bound-variable renaming admitted by canonical form changes neither identity; changing a captured declaration, branch, premise, rule interpretation or proof step changes the appropriate key. Equivalent conclusions proved by different routes are not silently the same proof lambda, and extensional equality of functions is not canonical content equality (`def:nrz:reuse-content-keys`).

**Rule (When statement-only retention is sound)** · `rule:nrz:reuse-statement-key`

A key described as conclusion-only is sound for selecting existing authority only when it means the complete K_J, including premises and immutable judgment dependencies, and the consumer accepts any independently checked proof of that judgment. It is not sound to key just the displayed conclusion while omitting its context or conditions. The selected node's own derivation and dependency closure must still authenticate; the index supplies no substitute proof. If the operation asks whether particular supplied proof bytes check, or promises the bytes of that particular witness, K_J alone cannot skip checking them: it could accept an altered invalid proof because a valid proof of the conclusion was retained. That operation requires K_π or an equally strong exact proof-byte identity and receiving-owner authentication. Statement equality alone promises neither identical chosen proof bytes nor the same proof path (`def:nrz:reuse-lambda-equality`), (`[CAS-thm:cas:lemma-cache]`).

**Rule (Applying retained authority to premises)** · `rule:nrz:reuse-application`

Applying L supplies a capture-avoiding typed substitution σ for Δ and checked evidence e_i for each instantiated premise P_iσ, in order and in the required context, domain and stratum. The application checker authenticates L, checks substitution and premise compatibility, and concludes exactly Jσ with all remaining hypotheses visible. New premise proofs may establish the same required judgments, but their own identities belong to the new application's evidence when proof bytes are observed. A changed premise judgment is admissible only through an explicit parameter of the checked lambda or a separately checked transport; a ground cache entry cannot silently generalize. An application is a citation when it references the already checked body and pays for these boundary checks without rerunning b's derivation. Independently checking b in a new owner or reconstructing its omitted derivation is preparation or replay, even if it reaches the same judgment (`def:nrz:reuse-lambda`), (`rule:nrz:reuse-evidence-bytes`).

**Example (An exact scale is the target of its own lemma)** · `ex:nrz:reuse-exact-scale`

Normalized exact scale content can identify a root subjudgment because that judgment's input is an already checked positive exact constant. It does not identify the original physical expression that produced that constant, or bypass exponent, kind and role checks for a new descriptor. Thus the normalized-content key of (`[BINDINGS-def:bindings:scale-root-cache]`) and the original-expression key of (`[CAS-rule:cas:constant-cache-key]`) describe different exact claims and obey the same dependency rule. Equal approximate centres identify neither.

## Graph structure, publication and invariants · `sec:nrz:reuse-graph`

**Definition (Authority graph and citation)** · `def:nrz:reuse-authority-graph`

Let G contain immutable authority nodes, their exact statements and payloads, and typed dependency and evidence edges. Write `d →dep a` when a depends on d, and `c →cite l` when consumer c cites checked lemma l; the notation states roles, not a required storage orientation. A lemma node owns one checked payload. Its authenticated evidence edge records the judgment and permitted premise interface through which that payload is consumed. The structural index contains keys and references to nodes, not a competing store of truth. Schema and encoding nodes have their distinct authority kinds, and provenance nodes retain owners through explicit references. A new application can add a citation edge without copying or rechecking the cited derivation (`[CAS-def:cas:anonymous-judgment-node]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:anonymous-judgment-evidence]`).

**Rule (Owner and generation qualify a node)** · `rule:nrz:reuse-node-lifetime`

A local reusable reference resolves through an authenticated owner, namespace generation and node identity. Authentication checks that the owner retains the checked node and evidence edge and that its namespace has not been invalidated; content equality alone cannot authenticate a foreign handle. Before rollback permits logical identifiers to recur, the relevant generation advances, and saturation disables reuse instead of wrapping. Discarding an unpublished suffix that has no assigned identities need not pretend that committed nodes were rolled back. A registry slot or immutable proof owner can have a different retention lifetime from a CAS graph, but it must declare that lifetime and prevent stale references from selecting a replacement. Private owner tokens qualify authority; they do not become semantic address keys or portable witness fields (`[CAS-rule:cas:shared-judgment-ledger]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:anonymous-judgment-locality]`), (`[BINDINGS-dec:bindings:scale-root-capacity]`).

**Definition (Reuse transaction boundary)** · `def:nrz:reuse-transaction`

A reuse transaction T names its owner, staged authority nodes, evidence edges, index entries and storage reservations, together with the final fallible checks required before publication. Commit admits that entire declared suffix; refusal or exhaustion removes its unpublished additions and speculative capacity, preserves earlier committed authority and retains all spent Work. Nested work shares the declared enclosing attempt when publication depends on its consumer. Independently committed preparation belongs to a separate transaction and is not undone merely because a later consumer fails. Private reuse of already checked staged authority within the same enclosing attempt must retain its scope and roll back all dependent additions together. No staged node becomes reusable outside that transaction before its authority and publication boundary complete (`[CAS-rule:cas:anonymous-judgment-rollback]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:shared-judgment-attempt]`).

**Example (Preparation and selection have different commits)** · `ex:nrz:reuse-selection-boundary`

Widened lattice revalidation may commit independently checked preparation lemmas, while the subsequent selection transaction must roll back every new selected record on refusal or undecided. A previously retired native attachment remains retired; valid archived theorem evidence cannot publish it again. Scale-root preparation instead remains staged through the numerical consumer, and published moment provenance appears only on successful publication. These scopes are compatible instances of (`def:nrz:reuse-transaction`), not a universal rule that any unsuccessful outer request leaves every cache cold (`dec:nrz:lattice-widened-revalidation`), (`[BINDINGS-rule:bindings:scale-root-publication]`), (`[MATRIX-rule:matrix:quantity-covariance-proof-reuse]`).

**Rule (Dependency invalidation)** · `rule:nrz:reuse-invalidation`

If a dependency's required identity, version, policy or authority is no longer available, each dependent use loses eligibility along `→dep` edges and each citation requiring it must be refused, rechecked or reconstructed. Propagation follows the complete closure of the claim and can conservatively invalidate more uses. Immutable old content is not rewritten: its theorem remains valid about its original premises while retained, but it cannot justify changed premises without a checked application or transport. Numerical mutation invalidates dependent numerical artifacts and live attachments; it need not invalidate a purely symbolic lemma that has no numerical-version dependency. Evicting a lookup entry removes a route to authority without revoking independently retained published results. Authentication and complete dependency matching are required even if invalidation is detected lazily (`rule:nrz:descriptor-lifetime-and-mutation`), (`def:nrz:reuse-local-lifetime`), (`def:nrz:reuse-live-lifetime`).

**Rule (Semantic validity invariant)** · `rule:nrz:reuse-semantic-validity`

For identical target, premises and rule interpretation, citation proves the same judgment as the cited checked authority; application proves its checked substitution under (`rule:nrz:reuse-application`). Reuse cannot strengthen a conditional conclusion, drop a domain guard or change the truth of a completed proved or refuted verdict. This invariant concerns the completed mathematical claim, not whether a finite search finds it, which proof is chosen, or whether a resource allowance suffices. Refusal, unsupported, undecided and exhaustion remain distinct from mathematical refutation (`rule:nrz:cas-search-bounds`).

**Rule (Evidence-byte invariant and its scope)** · `rule:nrz:reuse-evidence-bytes`

An authenticated citation borrows the immutable judgment and payload of its target node, so it preserves that cited judgment and those bytes exactly. It does not rewrite the node to reflect a consumer's new premises. Canonical export of a fixed proof/declaration under a fixed format, roots, revisions and required identities has identical bytes; local owner/generation authentication is outside portable bytes. A new application's wrapper, substitution or premise proofs can change its full witness bytes while leaving the cited payload unchanged. Equality of statements, bound validity and reuse alone do not imply equality between alternative witnesses. A mechanism promising cold/warm byte identity must pin the same proof through a proof-byte key or establish that its two admitted routes select the identical canonical records for the same fresh identities (`def:nrz:reuse-lambda-equality`), (`def:nrz:cas-design-witness-format`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:anonymous-judgment-locality]`), (`[BINDINGS-rule:bindings:quantity-registration-freshness]`).

**Rule (Bound-validity invariant)** · `rule:nrz:reuse-bound-validity`

A reused bound must remain valid for the identical target, region, premises and covered error components. A service may independently check a refinement or intersect compatible valid enclosures and return a tighter valid bound; the refinement is new admitted evidence and never a mutation of the cited bound's theorem. Identical endpoints are a stronger contract than validity, as is preservation of one certificate's bytes. A tighter bound may settle a predicate that the earlier bound left undecided without changing any completed mathematical verdict (`rule:nrz:cas-enclosures-target`), (`rule:nrz:cas-enclosures-refinement`), (`rule:nrz:reuse-semantic-validity`).

**Rule (Numerical reproducibility invariant)** · `rule:nrz:reuse-numerical-reproducibility`

For matched numerical inputs, coordinates and declared arithmetic/error policy, reuse must preserve the applicable numerical accuracy guarantee and every reported error enclosure. Two permitted evaluations need not have identical floating bits; they must meet their respective bounds for the same mathematical target, and their difference must be covered by the corresponding combined error in the declared comparison norm. Statistical uncertainty is not a deterministic arithmetic error bound. Bitwise reproducibility requires a separately fixed arithmetic schedule, representation and rounding contract. Reusing a symbolic derivative does not skip fresh numerical Jacobian evaluation, current operand capture, error accounting or source contraction (`def:nrz:reuse-numerical-artifact`), (`[MATRIX-rule:matrix:quantity-covariance-proof-reuse]`).

**Example (A reusable part does not certify a new whole)** · `ex:nrz:reuse-part-and-whole`

An eligible factor of an unchanged operator can serve a changed right-hand side, but the new solve still needs its own candidate, residual, perturbation bound and budget decision. A derivative identity over unchanged descriptors can serve another operating point, but the old numerical Jacobian and covariance certificate cannot be reused on that basis. In both cases the retained part has a smaller dependency closure than the new whole (`rule:nrz:computation-cache-validity`), (`rule:nrz:computation-result-cache`), (`[MATRIX-rule:matrix:quantity-covariance-proof-reuse]`).

## Work and deterministic replay · `sec:nrz:reuse-work-and-determinism`

**Definition (Hit and miss)** · `def:nrz:reuse-hit-miss`

A hit is a complete identity match to currently authenticated, eligible retained authority under the caller's active ceilings. It skips only the preparation represented by that authority. Absence or incompatibility is a miss when the service permits independent preparation; malformed, foreign or stale handles can instead require explicit refusal. A miss performs the full ordinary checking/preparation before any new authority is admitted. Neither a lookup result nor a partial failed preparation counts as a hit (`def:nrz:reuse-index`), (`def:nrz:reuse-transaction`).

**Rule (Charge performed work and retained storage)** · `rule:nrz:reuse-work`

Write `W_hit = W_key + W_lookup + W_auth + W_apply + W_current + W_copy`, with `W_lookup ≥ 1`, and `W_miss = W_key + W_probe + W_prepare + W_admit + W_current + W_copy` for a miss including its lookup, full independent preparation and publication. These are categories of actual work, not additional tariffs on top of an allowance that already covers the same operations; each performed operation is charged once. A mechanism can combine categories in a conservative charge but must name what it covers. Keys, complete comparisons, premise matching, current containment, output copies and fresh numerical work remain bounded and charged when performed; skipped derivation work is not charged. The caller's cumulative ledger includes failed attempts and never refunds spent Work at rollback. Every active retained key, payload, index and owner-registration reservation remains within the applicable caller/store storage ceilings beside scratch and staged additions. A hit can therefore refuse for storage or remaining Work despite mathematically valid retained evidence (`[CAS-rule:cas:anonymous-judgment-accounting]`), (`[MATRIX-rule:matrix:quantity-covariance-caller-accounting]`).

**Definition (Initial reuse state and admission history)** · `def:nrz:reuse-replay-state`

Let Q contain ordered sources and declarations, exact context and goal, rule/checker/encoding revisions, search and arithmetic policy, budgets and prior cumulative usage. Let S₀ be the complete relevant initial graph and reuse state: authenticated owner relationships and generations, logical identities and next admission counters, retained checked nodes and proofs, index contents and deterministic iteration/probe order, publication and eviction order, and retained/reserved resource usage. Let H be the ordered history of admissions, commits, rollbacks, evictions, replacements and externally supplied changes relevant to those owners before or during evaluation. History already represented completely by S₀ need not be duplicated, but any effect consulted by selection or accounting must be fixed. Concurrent owners require a declared deterministic admission schedule or a fixed recorded order; lock safety alone does not choose that order. Private owner tokens may be renamed only under a correspondence preserving all authentication relationships and logical identities, since they are not portable content (`rule:nrz:reuse-node-lifetime`).

**Rule (Determinism is relative to reuse state)** · `rule:nrz:reuse-determinism`

A bounded deterministic evaluator has a fixed result and state transition `Run(Q, S₀, H)` for the same Q, S₀ and relevant H under the declared total orders. Within that scope its resource outcome, admitted logical graph, selected path, witness and serialized response agree. Comparing different reuse states or admission histories has no such whole-run guarantee: costs, completion, selected proofs and graph contents can differ. Completed mathematical judgments preserve (`rule:nrz:reuse-semantic-validity`), bounds preserve (`rule:nrz:reuse-bound-validity`), fixed evidence preserves (`rule:nrz:reuse-evidence-bytes`), and numerical results preserve (`rule:nrz:reuse-numerical-reproducibility`). These separate invariants do not imply arbitrary cross-store or admission-history byte equivalence. Wall-clock timing and external interruption supply no mathematical verdict (`rule:nrz:cas-search-bounds`), (`rule:nrz:cas-enclosures-determinism`).

**Example (Warm completion under the same allowance)** · `ex:nrz:reuse-warm-completion`

The direct `2*Pi` fixture costs 27,938 Work for cold preparation and one Work for a retained citation (`[CAS-ex:cas:constant-cache-costs]`). With adequate other resources and one available Work, the warm request can finish while the cold request exhausts. Both routes, when completed, establish the same checked constant judgment, and the retained service preserves its specified witness bytes. The requests differ in S₀, so their different bounded outcomes agree with (`rule:nrz:reuse-determinism`). For eligible repeated preparation the cost is one preparation plus the metered citation and remaining consumer work, not two preparations; eviction or invalidation can require a new preparation.

## Mechanism instances and open requirements · `sec:nrz:reuse-instances`

**Definition (Prepared constants and local anonymous judgments)** · `def:nrz:reuse-constant-instance`

Prepared exact constants instantiate (`def:nrz:reuse-lemma`) and (`def:nrz:reuse-local-lifetime`); their graph index instantiates (`def:nrz:reuse-index`). The key fixes the judgment kind/revision and original expression/context closure within authenticated CAS ownership and rollback generation. The graph owns the preparation and checked-evidence edge. A miss performs ordinary closed-constant checking and staged publication; a hit authenticates the edge and borrows the original preparation with a nonzero lookup charge. Inline finite-real identities borrow it without new portable authority. Enclosing graph refusal removes new judgments and index entries, preserves earlier evidence and spent Work, and invalidates a rolled-back namespace before identifier reuse. Witness export and independent replay retain their original bytes and checks (`[CAS-def:cas:constant-cache]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:constant-cache-key]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:constant-cache-identities]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:constant-cache-lifecycle]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:constant-graph-publication]`).

**Definition (Portable replay retention)** · `def:nrz:reuse-portable-instance`

Portable anonymous-lemma retention combines (`def:nrz:reuse-portable-lifetime`) with a caller-owned locally checked result under (`def:nrz:reuse-local-lifetime`). Its key includes the complete canonical expected judgment and proof bytes, qualified by receiving-owner authentication. A hit checks that owner and compares complete content while skipping rule replay and graph publication; a miss meters full replay and retained payload before admitting a sealed result. Failed or exhausted replay adds no entry; changed proof bytes cannot hit an earlier entry and require independent replay before admission. Another receiving store checks its own entries. Thus this is proof-key retention, distinct from a statement key omitting derivation. The specified cache owns its sealed witness; this concept does not assert that it uses the same physical index or publication boundary as graph-owned constant judgments. The explicitly labelled lifecycle and cost evidence still required is (`req:nrz:reuse-portable-lifecycle`), (`req:nrz:reuse-portable-cost-evidence`), under (`[CAS-thm:cas:lemma-cache]`).

**Definition (Checked scalar inventories)** · `def:nrz:reuse-scalar-instance`

Scalar record retention is a graph-owned anonymous lemma with a nonauthoritative structural index. Its full canonical certificate key includes revision, original expression, context, input family, ordered laws, native sources, exact endpoints and earlier-record references, qualified by store and generation. The retained claim is the reconstructed meaning of that inventory, not permission to change original target or evidence origin. A hit charges key/probe/authentication work and skips that reconstruction; original-target, scope, native-set and endpoint checks still run. A miss runs independent reconstruction and stages publication through the enclosing receipt/consumer's final copy and containment. Refused additions disappear without refunding Work; canonical receipt bytes remain unchanged (`[CAS-def:cas:scalar-record-cache]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:scalar-record-cache-key]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:scalar-record-cache-accounting]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:shared-judgment-attempt]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:scalar-graph-publication]`).

**Definition (Checked finite Machin certificates)** · `def:nrz:reuse-machin-instance`

Machin retention is a graph-owned anonymous lemma for the exact finite certificate. Its key contains theorem revision, both term counts, exact partial sums and declared bounds, with authenticated store and generation. The theorem has no context assumption; the graph retains its original consumer dependency, and every new consumer still checks its own scope, original target, interval reduction and containment. A hit charges complete-key lookup and authenticates the retained evidence edge instead of repeating finite checking. A miss independently checks and stages the node with the complete enclosing suffix; refusal removes its speculative capacity and keeps spent Work. Equal approximate endpoints cannot replace this content identity (`[CAS-def:cas:pi-bound-cache]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:pi-bound-cache-key]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:pi-graph-publication]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:pi-bound-cache-accounting]`).

**Definition (Registry scale roots)** · `def:nrz:reuse-scale-instance`

Scale-root retention is a registry-owned checked exact subjudgment with a bounded lookup, not a current numerical artifact. It keys checked positive normalized scale content and the effective CAS policy except cumulative generation Work; exponent, kind and role checks precede citation. Four slots use fixed insertion order and oldest-insertion eviction, clones start empty, and a stale pending generation prevents insertion. Hits pay policy/content comparisons and result-copy work while preserving exact result evidence; misses use full exact root preparation. New entries remain staged through descriptor or numerical-consumer publication, and eviction never revokes a descriptor that already owns its result. The exact unit-scale identity is a separately checked inline route without an entry (`[BINDINGS-def:bindings:scale-root-cache]`), (`[BINDINGS-rule:bindings:scale-root-preparation]`), (`[BINDINGS-rule:bindings:scale-root-publication]`), (`[BINDINGS-dec:bindings:scale-root-capacity]`), (`[BINDINGS-ex:bindings:scale-root-costs]`).

**Definition (Frozen signatures and header-key segments)** · `def:nrz:reuse-signature-instance`

A validated immutable declaration environment supplies (`def:nrz:reuse-schema`) and its canonical signature supplies (`def:nrz:reuse-encoding`). The complete header key compares concatenated canonical bytes, including actual fresh scalar-space references; a shared allocation is not a key based on addresses. The environment owns the signature and graph headers, index and journal retain that owner. First freezing validates and encodes the environment; reuse skips repeated table checks and signature copies, but every fresh admission still pays current policy/reference validation, shared-reference acquisition, fresh framing and complete-key probing. Failed admission rolls back new keys and records together. Matching independent ordinary admission produces the same canonical declaration bytes, without granting value, arithmetic or bound authority (`[CAS-rule:cas:frozen-environment-admission]`), (`[CAS-dec:cas:registration-key-segment]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:registration-key-segment-accounting]`).

**Definition (Whole-registration recipe contract)** · `def:nrz:reuse-registration-instance`

The planned whole-registration recipe is bindings-owned (`def:nrz:reuse-schema`) retained by the frozen quantity declaration snapshot and referring to actual admitted graph records. Its compatibility key fixes the snapshot/revision, complete descriptor including kind, role, measure, origin and count domain, ordered shape, companion inventory, non-Work policy and recipe revision. Owner/generation authentication and a bounded retained slot qualify hits; replacement releases only that reference while published symbols retain their owners. A warm route pays compatibility, containment, fresh identity/relation construction, encoding and publication; a miss pays full schema/header preparation as well. The complete new suffix and recipe remain staged through the numerical consumer. Both routes must produce independent-admission bytes for the same preselected fresh identities. Signature sharing alone does not implement this recipe (`[BINDINGS-def:bindings:quantity-registration-authority]`), (`[BINDINGS-rule:bindings:quantity-registration-freshness]`), (`[BINDINGS-rule:bindings:quantity-registration-attempt]`), (`dec:nrz:quantity-registration-owner`), (`rule:nrz:quantity-registration-accounting`), (`req:nrz:reuse-registration-evidence`).

**Definition (Published quantity sensitivities)** · `def:nrz:reuse-sensitivity-instance`

Published quantity sensitivities are bindings-owned checked symbolic lemmas retained through ordinary immutable moment provenance. Their key fixes original frozen rule authority, closed recipe revision including its exact exponent where applicable, complete non-Work symbolic policy and exact ordered input/output descriptors. Numerical values, versions, precision and source models are outside this symbolic identity. A bounded published-record search pays lookup and authentication, then every hit performs fresh operand/source capture, numerical Jacobian evaluation, error enclosure and covariance contraction. A miss additionally charges cumulative symbolic admission, construction, generation and replay to the original caller. Only successful moment publication supplies an entry; stale numerical outputs do not invalidate the retained symbolic theorem. The mechanism's verdict, bound and canonical witness preservation applies to the reused identity under matched premises, not to an old numerical result at a new operating point (`[MATRIX-rule:matrix:quantity-covariance-proof-reuse]`), (`[MATRIX-rule:matrix:quantity-covariance-caller-accounting]`), (`rule:nrz:reuse-evidence-bytes`), (`rule:nrz:reuse-numerical-reproducibility`).

**Requirement (Portable retention lifecycle completion)** · `req:nrz:reuse-portable-lifecycle`

The complete judgment/proof-byte key, receiving-owner authentication, caller ownership and refusal atomicity are specified by (`[CAS-thm:cas:lemma-cache]`). Its owning design must additionally label the exact invalidation behavior after receiving-store rollback, destruction and namespace exhaustion, the lifetime of surviving sealed witnesses, and whether an enclosing consumer failure is inside the cache's replay transaction or follows an independently committed replay. Each case must name what remains owned, what can still hit, and which new entries roll back under (`def:nrz:reuse-transaction`). A graph-cache lifecycle must not be silently inferred for this caller-owned cache; these are specification-completion obligations, not assertions of a demonstrated unsound implementation.

**Requirement (Portable retention cost evidence)** · `req:nrz:reuse-portable-cost-evidence`

The metering contract of (`[CAS-thm:cas:lemma-cache]`) must be completed with labelled lookup-growth and retained-storage accounting and independently reviewed literal cold/warm Work fixtures. Preserve the discovered-threshold enforcement property of (`[CAS-test:unit:lemma-cache-work-boundaries]`), but add literal N and N−1 evidence with prior cumulative Work, complete-key changes, owner changes and late refusal preserving earlier entries. An implementation-derived threshold alone does not detect a simultaneous increase in both measured costs and expectations. The owning evidence must state the number/order of entries visited and the publication scope it measures; this concept supplies no new literal pin or test change.

**Requirement (Common retained registration accounting completion)** · `req:nrz:reuse-registration-account`

The graph-owned retained count and payload already include constant judgments; (`[CAS-rule:cas:shared-judgment-ledger]`) separately leaves consolidation of constant callers into the common registration account as an obligation of (`[CAS-rule:cas:anonymous-judgment-accounting]`). Completion must specify which admission/citation ledger reserves each shared payload and registration component beside scratch, then provide mixed-kind evidence covering constants, scalar inventories, Machin certificates and every additional retained judgment kind using that account. Include warm citations and refused enclosing attempts with earlier retained payload and monotone Work. This distinguishes an unfinished common account from an unsupported claim that constant callers omit all other retained bytes, and avoids treating the register as permanently limited to three kinds.

**Requirement (Whole-registration implementation evidence)** · `req:nrz:reuse-registration-evidence`

The recipe identity, lifetime, transaction scope and charge categories are specified by (`def:nrz:reuse-registration-instance`); its implemented fast path and literal cold/warm costs remain obligations of (`req:nrz:quantity-registration-boundaries`). Evidence must compare independent ordinary admission of the same fresh declaration, distinct value/companion identities on successive hits, owner/policy incompatibility, active containment ceilings and numerical failure after symbol preparation. Shared header bytes alone satisfy none of these unmeasured recipe-instantiation claims.

